# Tutorial 06 — Audio and Music
# [bgm], [sfx], [voice], [synthesizer], and audio fading commands.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_06_audio
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Audio has four layers in VNS: background music, sound effects, voice clips, and procedural synthesis.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Each layer has its own commands. Let's go through them.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
> [bgm] — background music -> section_bgm
> [sfx] — sound effects -> section_sfx
> [voice] — voice acting -> section_voice
> [synthesizer] — procedural ambience -> section_synth
> Fading and crossfading audio -> section_fading
> Practice: scoring a scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── BGM ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_bgm
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}[bgm "path"]{/b} starts a music track that loops until you stop or replace it.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bgm "assets/audio/bgm/peaceful_morning.mp3"]{/color}
narrator: The track plays immediately and loops continuously.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: To switch tracks, call [bgm] again — the old track stops, the new one starts:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bgm "assets/audio/bgm/tense_night.mp3"]{/color}
Lavender: To stop music entirely:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bgm_stop]{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Format: MP3 or OGG. Keep music files in {b}assets/audio/bgm/{/b} and name them descriptively — cafe_jazz_loop.mp3, not track_03.mp3.
> Next: sfx -> section_sfx
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── SFX ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_sfx
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}[sfx "path"]{/b} plays a one-shot sound. It fires once and stops — no looping.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[sfx "assets/audio/sfx/door_close.wav"]{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Timing matters. Place the SFX command {i}before{/i} the dialogue line that reacts to it:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[sfx "assets/audio/sfx/glass_break.wav"]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: What was that?!{/color}
narrator: The player hears the crash, then reads the reaction. Sound first, words second.
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: SFX files should be short — 0.5 to 2 seconds. Use WAV for crisp, instant playback.
Lavender: Folder: {b}assets/audio/sfx/{/b}. Names: door_close.wav, footstep_wood.wav, thunder_distant.wav.
> Next: voice -> section_voice
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Voice ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_voice
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}[voice "path"]{/b} plays a voice clip alongside the next dialogue line.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[voice "assets/audio/voice/lavender_001.wav"]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: I can't believe it's already sunset.{/color}
narrator: The clip starts when the line appears. If the player clicks ahead, the clip stops.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Voice acting is optional. If you use it:
narrator: {b}Naming:{/b} characterId_lineNumber.wav — lavender_001.wav, alex_042.wav.
narrator: {b}Folder:{/b} assets/audio/voice/ with subfolders per character.
narrator: {b}Settings:{/b} always give players a separate voice volume slider.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Voice files add up fast. A 10-hour VN can have thousands of clips. Organize early.
> Next: synthesizer -> section_synth
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Synthesizer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_synth
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The {b}[synthesizer]{/b} command generates ambient sound procedurally — no audio files needed.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[synthesizer on mode:"rain" intensity:0.7 volume:0.4 loop:true]{/color}
narrator: Starts a rain ambience at 70% intensity, 40% volume, looping.
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Available modes and their key parameters:
narrator: {b}wind{/b} — {color=#8888cc}[synthesizer on mode:"wind" intensity:0.7 volume:0.35 loop:true]{/color}
narrator: {b}rain{/b} — {color=#8888cc}[synthesizer on mode:"rain" intensity:0.68 detail:0.82 motion:0.35 spread:0.72 accent:0.9]{/color}
narrator: {b}thunder{/b} — {color=#8888cc}[synthesizer on mode:"thunder" intensity:0.8 accent:0.7]{/color}
narrator: {b}fireplace{/b} — {color=#8888cc}[synthesizer on mode:"fireplace" intensity:0.6 detail:0.75]{/color}
narrator: {b}night_insects{/b} — {color=#8888cc}[synthesizer on mode:"night_insects" intensity:0.5 accent:0.8 motion:0.6]{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Parameters: {b}intensity{/b} (strength), {b}detail{/b} (texture), {b}motion{/b} (stereo movement), {b}spread{/b} (stereo width), {b}accent{/b} (preset-specific variation).
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[synthesizer off]{/color}
narrator: Stops all active synth layers.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Synth ambience is zero-filesize and infinitely variable. Layer it under BGM for rich atmospheric scenes.
> Next: fading -> section_fading
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Fading ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_fading
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Abrupt music stops are jarring. Use fades instead:
narrator: {b}Fade out:{/b} {color=#8888cc}[bgm_fadeout 1200]{/color} — current track fades to silence over 1200ms.
narrator: {b}Fade in:{/b} {color=#8888cc}[bgm_fadein "path" 800]{/color} — new track starts silent, rises to full over 800ms.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: For a smooth crossfade between tracks, use both together:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bgm_fadeout 1000]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bgm_fadein "assets/audio/bgm/tense_night.mp3" 1000]{/color}
narrator: Both run simultaneously — old track fades out as new track fades in.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Fade durations: 600–1000ms for standard changes, 1200–2000ms for slow dramatic shifts, 300–500ms for urgent mood swaps.
> Practice: scoring a scene -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: I'll show you how audio commands layer into a real scene. Follow the script annotations.
[wait 300]

@label practice_scored_scene
{{BGM_START}}
narrator: {b}↑ [bgm] started a track above this line.{/b}
[wait 300]
narrator: Morning light filtered through the curtains. The town was still quiet.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Another beautiful day. I wonder what's out there.
[wait 200]
narrator: A knock at the door — sharp and sudden.
narrator: {b}↑ You'd add [sfx "knock.wav"] right before that narrator line.{/b}
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: Who's there...?
[wait 400]
narrator: Silence. Then the distant rumble of thunder.
narrator: {b}↑ Here you'd layer [synthesizer on mode:"thunder" intensity:0.5 accent:0.6] for atmosphere.{/b}
[wait 300]
{{BGM_FADE}}
narrator: {b}↑ [bgm_fadeout] faded the music to silence.{/b}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: That scene used four audio tools:
narrator: {b}1.{/b} [bgm] for the background music track.
narrator: {b}2.{/b} [sfx] for the door knock (marked where it would go).
narrator: {b}3.{/b} [synthesizer] for the thunder atmosphere.
narrator: {b}4.{/b} [bgm_fadeout] to fade music as the mood shifts.
Lavender: Add your own audio files and replace the annotations with real commands to hear it come alive.
> Replay the scored scene -> practice_scored_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_2]
